<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Later Guptas</h1><p class="st-meta">490 CE – 750 CE (approx.) · capital Pataliputra</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A dynasty of Magadha and Malwa, unrelated by any proven tie to the imperial Guptas, that contested north India with the Maukharis and Harsha.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a> appear in the late 5th and 6th centuries as rulers in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Magadha</a> and eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malwa">Malwa</a> after the decline of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/gupta-empire">Gupta Empire</a>. They are known mainly from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aphsad-inscription">Aphsad inscription</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adityasena">Adityasena</a>, which lists the line from its founder Krishnagupta. Despite the name, there is no proven connection to the imperial Guptas.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>For much of the 6th century the Later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gupta-empire">Guptas</a> fought the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/maukharis">Maukharis of Kannauj</a>; one king, Kumaragupta, defeated the Maukhari Ishanavarman. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mahasenagupta">Mahasenagupta</a> campaigned as far as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brahmaputra">Brahmaputra</a>, while <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madhavagupta">Madhavagupta</a> became a close ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harsha">Harsha</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/pushyabhuti-dynasty">Pushyabhuti dynasty</a>. After Harsha's death <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adityasena">Adityasena</a> revived the kingdom, performed a horse sacrifice and built temples.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The last known king, Jivitagupta II, was probably defeated in the early 8th century by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yashovarman-of-kannauj">Yashovarman of Kannauj</a>, whose victory over a king of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-magadha">Magadha</a> is celebrated in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> poem <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gaudavaho">Gaudavaho</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magadha">Magadha</a> later fell to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/pala-empire">Pala Empire</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/later-guptas.jpg" alt="Later Guptas" width="320" height="151"><figcaption>Image: Mahasenagupta of the Later Gupta dynasty · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Swordsman_type_coinage_of_Mahasenagupta_of_the_Later_Gupta_dynasty.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>490 CE – 750 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/5th-century">5th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Pataliputra</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Mahasenagupta, Madhavagupta, Adityasena</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Sanskrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Later Gupta dynasty, Guptas of Magadha</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=620&amp;focus=later-guptas" aria-label="Later Guptas on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6933 1236 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Later Guptas lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M7253 1492L7362 1550M7253 1492L7103 1442M7253 1492L7253 1492M7253 1492L7348 1548"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M7362 1550h0M7103 1442h0M7253 1492h0M7348 1548h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7253 1492h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7253 1492h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Pataliputra. <a href="/atlas?year=620&amp;focus=later-guptas">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
